# <Theme> — <what the rule covers, in three or four words>

<The rule's single principle, in one sentence. If you need two sentences to state it,
it's probably two rules.>

## Why this is a rule and not a <`CLAUDE.md` section | skill>

<Three sentences, no adjectives. Why it's a declarative fact with territory, and not a
universal fact or a procedure. The interview axis that motivated it. If there was a full
record, cite it: `@.claude/decisions/NNNN-<slug>.md`. Do NOT name skills or agents here —
invariant 1. If the justification needs to name them, it doesn't belong in this rule: it
lives only in the decision record.>

## <Subtheme>

- <Imperative, verifiable rule. "Zero framework in the domain," not "avoid frameworks.">
- <Rule with a concrete value. "Maximum 4 parameters," not "few parameters.">
- <When the rule has an exception, write the exception here, not in a footnote.>

## <Subtheme>

- <…>

## Admitted exception

<The single situation where the rule yields, and what has to happen for it to yield — an
ADR, an approval, a test. If there's no exception, delete the section; don't write
"none".>

## How to verify

```bash
<command that proves compliance — build, test, lint>
```

